  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1223 PM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nThere is an upper level low spinning its way across the Dakotas\nand moving southeast into southwestern Minnesota and Iowa. This\nis bringing increasing clouds to our region, and there will be\nsome light snowfall associated with this that will affect\nsouthwestern Minnesota. A stray flurry can't be ruled out around\nthe Brainerd Lakes, but otherwise this should completely miss us\nand leave us with dry weather this afternoon through tonight. At\nthe surface, winds will be light and generally from the south on\nthe west side of broad high pressure that will continue to\ninfluence our weather through Wednesday. Temperatures will warm\nup a bit on Wednesday with some sunshine expected as that upper\nlow moves to the southeast. Temperatures should warm above\nfreezing across the region.\n\nThe upper level low over our region will merge with a broader\nseries of troughs in the southeastern states and morph into a\nlarger upper level low. Surface lows and plenty of precip will\naffect the east coast through the later part of the week as a\nresult, and we will essentially be in-between systems in the\nNorthland through the end of the week. Expect a pretty stagnant\npattern with dry weather and temperatures warming up slightly\nthrough the end of the week. \n\nAn atmospheric river along the west coast will eventually spread\nsome moisture our way via a low pressure system that's looking\nto track almost due east just north of the Canadian border. Some\nrain and snow is likely with this, though we may just catch the\nsouthern edge of the precipitation. Among most ensembles, much\nof our region will remain dry or just see very light\nprecipitation amounts (just a few hundredths of an inch of\nliquid equivalent precipitation; maybe a few tenths of an inch\nof snow). In the less likely event that the low takes a more\nsoutherly track, places along the International Border could see\nsome slightly boosted amounts, but even with that, impacts\nshould be minimal. Precipitation type will likely be dependent\non time of day, with snow at night and rain or mixed\nprecipitation during the day for what few places see\nprecipitation this weekend.\n\nEarly next week, the upper level pattern is looking quasi-zonal\nwith potential for some weak upper level troughs to pass\nthrough. There may be some hit-or-miss chances for light rain or\nsnow, but as of now, impacts look pretty minimal and certainly\nno large storms are hinted at in the models at this time.",
